{"type": "say", "text": "Thank you, Dr. Patel. From the clinical side, I can confirm that the satellite ED does have a medication refrigerator in our acute care area that maintains temperature in the 2-8°C range — it's where we currently store other temperature-sensitive emergency medications. That could serve as the storage location for the kits. Regarding staff training, we haven't yet drafted a formal training plan, but I'm prepared to take responsibility for developing and conducting that training session for satellite ED staff before any kits are placed into service. Given the poison center protocol update emphasizing immediate availability, I'd strongly support moving forward with the approval now, with those implementation prerequisites — confirmed refrigerated storage location, documented training completion — recorded as commitments to be fulfilled before the kits go live. The clinical urgency here is real: in suspected smoke-inhalation cases with cyanide toxicity, every minute of delay retrieving medication from the main pharmacy could matter."}
